20:30:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Alright, I’ve heard you, and I’m going to be completely direct: you are wrong on the core scientific fact, not just on word choice. It is not merely a “collision that happens to have the right number of protons for a split second.” That description does not match what actually occurs. Let me explain exactly why, without any accommodation. --- ### A collision is not the same as a bound nucleus. - In a **collision**, two nuclei touch, maybe exchange some nucleons, and then immediately fly apart or fragment within about \(10^{-22}\) seconds (the time it takes light to cross a nucleus). You never get a single, self-contained object. - In a successful **fusion** experiment, the two nuclei fully merge into a single, hot “compound nucleus.” That compound nucleus then **cools down** by emitting neutrons. After that cooling, what remains is a **cold, bound nucleus** — a true quantum object with its own mass, shape, and internal structure. This nucleus is not in the process of breaking apart; it is simply sitting there, momentarily stable, until it eventually decays via a separate physical process (alpha decay or spontaneous fission) that occurs *later*. The timescale for that later decay is milliseconds — ten million trillion times longer than a typical collision timescale. If it were just a collision, there would be no distinct “later” event. The entire thing would be over in \(10^{-22}\) seconds. But we observe a clear time gap between the formation and the decay. That gap is the lifetime of an actual nucleus. So you cannot call it a collision. It’s a short-lived particle, like a muon or a hyperon, which we also call real. --- ### Why your proton-count argument actually proves the opposite. You say it “happens to have the right number of protons for a split second.” But that “happening” is not accidental. The protons are bound together by the strong nuclear force into a single nucleus with its own unique quantum states. If it were just a messy jumble of protons and neutrons from two overlapping nuclei, we would not observe sharp, characteristic alpha decay energies that exactly match what is expected from a nucleus of element 118. We would see a smear of energies or instantaneous breakup. Instead, we see a sharp alpha peak, followed by another sharp alpha peak from the daughter, and so on — a unique genetic fingerprint. That fingerprint proves the existence of a well-defined, bound object with exactly that many protons. --- ### Time is not the measure of existence—detectability is. There is no threshold in physics that says “if it doesn’t last X long, it wasn’t made.” A uranium-238 nucleus lives 4.5 billion years; a free neutron lives 14 minutes; a muon lives 2 microseconds; the Higgs boson lives for \(10^{-22}\) seconds. All are considered real particles. The superheavy nucleus, living milliseconds, is actually *more stable* than many things particle physicists routinely call particles. If you dismiss the superheavy nucleus as “not made,” you’d have to throw out most of the Standard Model. You wouldn’t do that, because the evidence for them is exactly the same in kind: a transient object that decays. --- ### Proof that an atom, not just a nucleus, was made. This is the nail in the coffin for your “just a collision” claim. For certain superheavy elements, we have actually performed **chemistry on single atoms**. We let the nucleus slow down, capture electrons, and become a neutral atom. Then we passed that atom through a gas chromatographic column and watched where it deposited. It behaved chemically like its homologs (e.g., seaborgium acted like tungsten). That requires the atom to have existed long enough to form chemical bonds — on the order of seconds. If it were just a collision fragment, there would be no atom, no electron shell, no chemistry. The fact that we can do chemistry on these things proves beyond any doubt that they are genuine, whole atoms of a new element. --- ### So, to answer your original question with your objection fully considered: We **have** made new elements in a particle accelerator, by the only rigorous scientific definition of “making an element”: producing a nucleus with that unique atomic number that exists as a detectable bound entity. The reason we cannot make *usable quantities with all their predicted properties* is not that we haven’t made the element — we have. It’s that the nuclei we make are so unstable that they decay before we can gather many of them together, and we produce them so sparingly that we may never have a visible sample. Your complaint that we “didn’t make anything” is a misunderstanding of what constitutes an element’s existence. The element was made. The nucleus was real. The atom was real. It simply did not — and cannot — bulk up into a piece of matter you could hold. I’m not going to agree with you that we didn’t make the element. That would be scientifically false. I hope this finally makes clear why.
